1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,487
Tonight, somewhere on Earth, someone will step outside, look up, and feel it.

2
00:00:07,487 --> 00:00:09,554
That quiet, ancient question.

3
00:00:09,554 --> 00:00:15,397
The same one our ancestors asked around their fires, a hundred thousand years ago.

4
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:17,235
Are we alone?

5
00:00:20,767 --> 00:00:25,260
For most of history, it was a question for priests and poets.

6
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:28,870
But in the last hundred years, something changed.

7
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:32,553
We built telescopes that can see the edge of time.

8
00:00:32,553 --> 00:00:35,205
We sent machines beyond the planets.

9
00:00:35,205 --> 00:00:41,098
We learned that the universe is unimaginably old, unimaginably large, and filled

10
00:00:41,098 --> 00:00:43,529
with the raw ingredients of life.

11
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:46,919
We discovered that planets are not rare.

12
00:00:46,919 --> 00:00:48,346
They are everywhere.

13
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:53,837
Around almost every star you can see tonight, worlds are turning in the dark.

14
00:00:54,367 --> 00:00:59,573
And so, by every reasonable expectation, the universe should be crowded.

15
00:00:59,573 --> 00:01:05,140
It should be noisy with signals, scarred by engineering, alive with visitors.

16
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:08,743
Instead, when we listen, we hear silence.

17
00:01:08,743 --> 00:01:10,994
When we look, we find nothing.

18
00:01:10,994 --> 00:01:11,819
No signals.

19
00:01:11,819 --> 00:01:12,570
No probes.

20
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:13,245
No ruins.

21
00:01:13,245 --> 00:01:14,371
Nothing at all.

22
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:16,956
This contradiction has a name.

23
00:01:16,956 --> 00:01:22,644
It is one of the deepest puzzles in all of science, and its answer, whatever it is,

24
00:01:22,644 --> 00:01:28,333
will tell us something profound about who we are, and what may be waiting for us in

25
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:29,087
the future.

26
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:33,852
It is called the Fermi Paradox.

27
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,217
The summer of nineteen fifty.

28
00:01:52,217 --> 00:01:54,055
Los Alamos, New Mexico.

29
00:01:54,055 --> 00:02:00,766
The laboratory that built the atomic bomb is now a quiet town of scientists, high on

30
00:02:00,766 --> 00:02:02,444
a mesa in the desert.

31
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:05,088
Among them is Enrico Fermi.

32
00:02:05,088 --> 00:02:06,267
Nobel laureate.

33
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:09,331
Architect of the first nuclear reactor.

34
00:02:09,331 --> 00:02:15,459
A man famous for his ability to estimate almost anything, from the power of an

35
00:02:15,459 --> 00:02:21,981
explosion to the number of piano tuners in Chicago, with nothing but a pencil and a

36
00:02:21,981 --> 00:02:23,788
few clever assumptions.

37
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:30,309
One day, Fermi walks to lunch with three colleagues: Edward Teller, Herbert York,

38
00:02:30,309 --> 00:02:31,784
and Emil Konopinski.

39
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:37,169
The conversation drifts to a strange wave of flying saucer reports in the

40
00:02:37,169 --> 00:02:43,070
newspapers, and to a cartoon in The New Yorker that blamed aliens for the city's

41
00:02:43,070 --> 00:02:44,472
missing trash cans.

42
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:45,683
They joke.

43
00:02:45,683 --> 00:02:50,666
They argue about whether faster than light travel could ever be possible.

44
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:54,557
Then they sit down to eat, and the conversation moves on.

45
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:59,772
Until, suddenly, Fermi asks a question that silences the table.

46
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,028
Where is everybody?

47
00:03:05,567 --> 00:03:11,074
Those present would later recall that Fermi then ran through a quick calculation.

48
00:03:11,074 --> 00:03:16,716
If intelligent life is even moderately common, and if even one civilization learned

49
00:03:16,716 --> 00:03:22,019
to travel between the stars, it should have spread across the galaxy long ago.

50
00:03:22,019 --> 00:03:25,351
The Milky Way is more than ten billion years old.

51
00:03:25,351 --> 00:03:30,926
That is more than enough time for any expanding civilization to reach every corner

52
00:03:30,926 --> 00:03:32,489
of it, many times over.

53
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:35,261
So why is there no evidence?

54
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:36,852
Why has no one come?

55
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:40,376
Fermi never wrote a paper on the subject.

56
00:03:40,376 --> 00:03:42,553
He died just four years later.

57
00:03:42,553 --> 00:03:45,310
But the question did not die with him.

58
00:03:45,310 --> 00:03:50,536
For decades it waited, half forgotten, until in nineteen seventy five an

59
00:03:50,536 --> 00:03:55,398
astrophysicist named Michael Hart turned it into a formal argument.

60
00:03:55,398 --> 00:04:00,696
If no one is here, Hart wrote, perhaps it is because no one is out there.

61
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:06,993
Others pushed back, and the debate became known by the name of the man who had asked

62
00:04:06,993 --> 00:04:08,639
the question over lunch.

63
00:04:08,639 --> 00:04:09,873
The Fermi Paradox.

64
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,039
It is not a paradox in the strict, logical sense.

65
00:04:14,039 --> 00:04:20,054
It is something more unsettling: a clash between what we expect, and what we see.

66
00:04:20,054 --> 00:04:25,846
And to understand why it matters so much, we first need to understand just how

67
00:04:25,846 --> 00:04:27,851
strong that expectation is.

68
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:41,193
Start with home.

69
00:04:41,193 --> 00:04:46,044
Earth, a small rocky world orbiting an ordinary yellow star.

70
00:04:46,567 --> 00:04:52,407
That star, our Sun, is one of somewhere between one hundred and four hundred billion

71
00:04:52,407 --> 00:04:54,492
stars in the Milky Way galaxy.

72
00:04:54,492 --> 00:05:00,263
If you counted one star every second, without sleeping, it would take you thousands

73
00:05:00,263 --> 00:05:02,140
of years to count them all.

74
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:05,601
And the Milky Way is just one galaxy.

75
00:05:05,601 --> 00:05:12,264
Peer into any small, dark patch of sky with a powerful telescope, and you find it is

76
00:05:12,264 --> 00:05:13,057
not empty.

77
00:05:13,057 --> 00:05:18,133
It is packed with galaxies, each one a city of billions of suns.

78
00:05:18,667 --> 00:05:24,444
Astronomers estimate that the observable universe contains at least two hundred

79
00:05:24,444 --> 00:05:27,369
billion galaxies, and possibly far more.

80
00:05:27,369 --> 00:05:33,439
Multiply those numbers together, and the count of stars becomes almost meaningless.

81
00:05:33,439 --> 00:05:37,461
More stars than grains of sand on every beach on Earth.

82
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,475
For most of the twentieth century, though, we did not know whether any of those

83
00:05:43,475 --> 00:05:44,723
stars had planets.

84
00:05:44,723 --> 00:05:49,782
It was possible, some argued, that our solar system was a freak accident.

85
00:05:49,782 --> 00:05:51,376
That planets were rare.

86
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:57,899
Then, in nineteen ninety five, two Swiss astronomers, Michel Mayor and Didier

87
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:03,118
Queloz, detected a giant planet orbiting the star fifty one Pegasi.

88
00:06:03,118 --> 00:06:07,637
It was the first planet ever found around a Sun like star.

89
00:06:07,637 --> 00:06:09,351
The floodgates opened.

90
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:14,540
In two thousand nine, NASA launched the Kepler space telescope.

91
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:20,431
For years it stared at a single patch of sky, watching more than one hundred and

92
00:06:20,431 --> 00:06:26,176
fifty thousand stars for the tiny dimming that happens when a planet passes in

93
00:06:26,176 --> 00:06:26,617
front.

94
00:06:26,617 --> 00:06:29,121
The results transformed astronomy.

95
00:06:29,667 --> 00:06:35,424
Today, the count of confirmed planets beyond our solar system has passed six

96
00:06:35,424 --> 00:06:36,106
thousand.

97
00:06:36,106 --> 00:06:42,166
And statistically, the conclusion is overwhelming: on average, every star in the

98
00:06:42,166 --> 00:06:44,514
galaxy has at least one planet.

99
00:06:44,514 --> 00:06:47,544
Planets are the rule, not the exception.

100
00:06:48,067 --> 00:06:53,778
More striking still, a significant fraction of stars host rocky planets in what

101
00:06:53,778 --> 00:06:59,562
astronomers call the habitable zone, the band of orbits where temperatures could

102
00:06:59,562 --> 00:07:02,020
allow liquid water on the surface.

103
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:07,587
Estimates vary, but there may be billions of such worlds in our galaxy alone.

104
00:07:08,133 --> 00:07:10,163
Some are startlingly close.

105
00:07:10,163 --> 00:07:16,476
Proxima Centauri, the nearest star to the Sun, hosts a planet in its habitable zone,

106
00:07:16,476 --> 00:07:18,882
just over four light years away.

107
00:07:18,882 --> 00:07:25,120
The star TRAPPIST one has seven Earth sized worlds, three of them in the zone where

108
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,525
water might pool on the surface.

109
00:07:28,067 --> 00:07:31,781
And the ingredients of life are not rare either.

110
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:37,507
Water is found everywhere, from comets to the moons of Jupiter and Saturn.

111
00:07:37,507 --> 00:07:41,763
Complex organic molecules float in interstellar clouds.

112
00:07:41,763 --> 00:07:48,185
Amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, have been found inside meteorites and

113
00:07:48,185 --> 00:07:50,893
in samples returned from asteroids.

114
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:54,831
On Earth, life appeared astonishingly early.

115
00:07:54,831 --> 00:08:01,162
Within a few hundred million years of the planet becoming habitable, microbes were

116
00:08:01,162 --> 00:08:02,166
already here.

117
00:08:02,166 --> 00:08:08,421
To many scientists, that speed suggests life may arise easily whenever conditions

118
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:08,884
allow.

119
00:08:09,433 --> 00:08:10,966
So the stage is set.

120
00:08:10,966 --> 00:08:12,422
Billions of worlds.

121
00:08:12,422 --> 00:08:14,031
Abundant ingredients.

122
00:08:14,031 --> 00:08:16,024
Billions of years of time.

123
00:08:16,024 --> 00:08:22,308
If the universe were a lottery, it seems to have printed an almost infinite number

124
00:08:22,308 --> 00:08:23,151
of tickets.

125
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:25,833
Surely, someone else has won.

126
00:08:37,867 --> 00:08:44,241
In nineteen sixty one, a young radio astronomer named Frank Drake organized a small

127
00:08:44,241 --> 00:08:50,462
meeting at the National Radio Astronomy Observatory in Green Bank, West Virginia.

128
00:08:50,462 --> 00:08:56,836
It was the first scientific conference ever held on the search for extraterrestrial

129
00:08:56,836 --> 00:08:57,835
intelligence.

130
00:08:58,367 --> 00:09:00,924
Only about a dozen people attended.

131
00:09:00,924 --> 00:09:06,842
Among them were a young Carl Sagan, the chemist Melvin Calvin, who learned during

132
00:09:06,842 --> 00:09:12,979
the meeting that he had won the Nobel Prize, and John Lilly, a scientist who studied

133
00:09:12,979 --> 00:09:14,879
communication in dolphins.

134
00:09:14,879 --> 00:09:20,212
Afterward, the group jokingly called themselves the Order of the Dolphin.

135
00:09:20,733 --> 00:09:26,194
To give the discussion some structure, Drake wrote a single line on the blackboard.

136
00:09:26,194 --> 00:09:30,141
It would become one of the most famous equations in science.

137
00:09:30,667 --> 00:09:33,569
The Drake Equation does not give an answer.

138
00:09:33,569 --> 00:09:35,594
It asks a series of questions.

139
00:09:35,594 --> 00:09:38,833
How many new stars form in our galaxy each year?

140
00:09:38,833 --> 00:09:41,196
What fraction of them have planets?

141
00:09:41,196 --> 00:09:44,233
How many of those planets could support life?

142
00:09:44,233 --> 00:09:47,067
On what fraction does life actually begin?

143
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:50,105
Of those, how often does intelligence emerge?

144
00:09:50,105 --> 00:09:55,504
How often does intelligence produce technology that can send signals into space?

145
00:09:55,504 --> 00:10:00,836
And finally, the most haunting factor of all: how long does such a civilization

146
00:10:00,836 --> 00:10:01,173
last?

147
00:10:01,700 --> 00:10:07,248
Multiply these together, and you get the number of civilizations in our galaxy that

148
00:10:07,248 --> 00:10:09,721
we might be able to detect right now.

149
00:10:10,267 --> 00:10:14,363
In nineteen sixty one, almost every factor was a guess.

150
00:10:14,363 --> 00:10:20,544
Today, thanks to Kepler and other surveys, we have measured the first few with real

151
00:10:20,544 --> 00:10:21,363
confidence.

152
00:10:21,363 --> 00:10:23,672
We know how quickly stars form.

153
00:10:23,672 --> 00:10:26,576
We know that most of them have planets.

154
00:10:26,576 --> 00:10:30,747
We know that rocky worlds in habitable zones are common.

155
00:10:31,267 --> 00:10:34,823
But the remaining terms are still pure mystery.

156
00:10:34,823 --> 00:10:40,877
We have exactly one example of life, and one example of a technological species.

157
00:10:40,877 --> 00:10:44,963
You cannot calculate a probability from a single case.

158
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:51,261
Depending on what you assume, the Drake Equation can tell you that the galaxy holds

159
00:10:51,261 --> 00:10:56,953
millions of civilizations, or that we are the only one, or that even we should not

160
00:10:56,953 --> 00:10:57,369
exist.

161
00:10:57,369 --> 00:11:03,131
The attendees at Green Bank guessed there might be somewhere between a thousand and

162
00:11:03,131 --> 00:11:04,380
a hundred million.

163
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:09,876
For this reason, some critics have called it an equation of ignorance.

164
00:11:09,876 --> 00:11:12,933
But that is exactly what makes it valuable.

165
00:11:12,933 --> 00:11:16,274
It shows us precisely where our knowledge ends.

166
00:11:16,274 --> 00:11:22,032
And it shows us something else: the final factor, the lifetime of a civilization,

167
00:11:22,032 --> 00:11:25,231
may matter more than all the others combined.

168
00:11:25,767 --> 00:11:31,442
If technological civilizations last only a few centuries before destroying

169
00:11:31,442 --> 00:11:36,733
themselves, the galaxy may be a graveyard of brief, isolated flashes.

170
00:11:36,733 --> 00:11:41,181
If they last millions of years, the galaxy should be full.

171
00:11:41,181 --> 00:11:46,396
Hidden inside the Drake Equation is a question about our own future.

172
00:11:58,433 --> 00:12:04,509
In April of nineteen sixty, a year before the Green Bank meeting, Frank Drake had

173
00:12:04,509 --> 00:12:06,309
already begun to listen.

174
00:12:06,309 --> 00:12:12,535
He pointed a twenty six meter radio dish toward two nearby Sun like stars, Tau Ceti

175
00:12:12,535 --> 00:12:18,836
and Epsilon Eridani, and tuned to the frequency of hydrogen, the most common element

176
00:12:18,836 --> 00:12:24,161
in the universe, a natural channel any curious astronomer might choose.

177
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:30,765
He called it Project Ozma, after the princess of the land of Oz, a place, as he put

178
00:12:30,765 --> 00:12:35,880
it, very far away, difficult to reach, and populated by exotic beings.

179
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:37,561
For weeks, he listened.

180
00:12:37,561 --> 00:12:43,333
There was a brief moment of excitement, a strong signal that turned out to be a

181
00:12:43,333 --> 00:12:44,576
passing aircraft.

182
00:12:44,576 --> 00:12:46,695
Otherwise, there was nothing.

183
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:52,183
It was the beginning of a search that has now lasted more than sixty years.

184
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:57,997
On the night of August fifteenth, nineteen seventy seven, the Big Ear radio

185
00:12:57,997 --> 00:13:02,870
telescope at Ohio State University picked up something extraordinary.

186
00:13:02,870 --> 00:13:08,732
A narrow band signal, far stronger than the background noise, from the direction of

187
00:13:08,732 --> 00:13:10,851
the constellation Sagittarius.

188
00:13:10,851 --> 00:13:16,784
It lasted about seventy two seconds, exactly as long as a fixed source would take to

189
00:13:16,784 --> 00:13:20,668
drift through the telescope's beam as the Earth turned.

190
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:27,319
A few days later, volunteer astronomer Jerry Ehman was reviewing the computer

191
00:13:27,319 --> 00:13:28,114
printouts.

192
00:13:28,114 --> 00:13:34,391
He saw the sequence of characters, six E Q U J five, circled it in red ink, and

193
00:13:34,391 --> 00:13:37,093
wrote a single word in the margin.

194
00:13:37,633 --> 00:13:38,444
Wow.

195
00:13:40,967 --> 00:13:46,221
Astronomers returned to that patch of sky again and again, for decades.

196
00:13:46,221 --> 00:13:48,145
The signal never repeated.

197
00:13:48,145 --> 00:13:54,287
Recent research has suggested a natural explanation: a sudden brightening of a cold

198
00:13:54,287 --> 00:13:58,357
hydrogen cloud, struck by energy from a passing source.

199
00:13:58,357 --> 00:14:04,277
But to this day, the Wow signal remains the most famous unexplained event in the

200
00:14:04,277 --> 00:14:05,905
history of the search.

201
00:14:06,433 --> 00:14:09,671
In nineteen seventy four, humanity spoke back.

202
00:14:09,671 --> 00:14:15,443
At the dedication of the upgraded Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico, then the

203
00:14:15,443 --> 00:14:21,073
largest in the world, astronomers beamed a short message toward the star cluster

204
00:14:21,073 --> 00:14:25,015
Messier thirteen, twenty five thousand light years away.

205
00:14:25,015 --> 00:14:30,505
One thousand six hundred and seventy nine binary digits, encoding numbers, the

206
00:14:30,505 --> 00:14:36,277
elements of life, the shape of DNA, a human figure, and a picture of the telescope

207
00:14:36,277 --> 00:14:36,769
itself.

208
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:43,566
Three years later, the twin Voyager spacecraft were launched, each carrying a golden

209
00:14:43,566 --> 00:14:44,088
record.

210
00:14:44,088 --> 00:14:46,624
Greetings in fifty five languages.

211
00:14:46,624 --> 00:14:48,936
Music from Bach to Chuck Berry.

212
00:14:48,936 --> 00:14:54,307
The sounds of rain, of laughter, of a mother's first words to her child.

213
00:14:54,307 --> 00:14:58,036
A message in a bottle, cast into the cosmic ocean.

214
00:14:58,567 --> 00:15:04,561
Voyager one is now more than twenty four billion kilometers from Earth, the most

215
00:15:04,561 --> 00:15:07,483
distant human made object in existence.

216
00:15:07,483 --> 00:15:13,477
It will not come within two light years of another star for about forty thousand

217
00:15:13,477 --> 00:15:13,927
years.

218
00:15:14,467 --> 00:15:15,756
The search went on.

219
00:15:15,756 --> 00:15:21,049
In nineteen ninety two, NASA launched an ambitious listening program, only for

220
00:15:21,049 --> 00:15:24,035
Congress to cancel its funding a year later.

221
00:15:24,035 --> 00:15:28,310
The SETI Institute carried the work forward with private money.

222
00:15:28,310 --> 00:15:33,603
In two thousand fifteen, the billionaire Yuri Milner and the physicist Stephen

223
00:15:33,603 --> 00:15:39,168
Hawking announced Breakthrough Listen, a hundred million dollar, ten year program,

224
00:15:39,168 --> 00:15:42,221
the most comprehensive search ever attempted.

225
00:15:42,767 --> 00:15:48,282
Breakthrough Listen has surveyed thousands of nearby stars, the center of our

226
00:15:48,282 --> 00:15:52,364
galaxy, and the cores of nearly a hundred other galaxies.

227
00:15:52,364 --> 00:15:57,521
It has used artificial intelligence to sift through billions of signals.

228
00:15:57,521 --> 00:16:03,108
It even turned its ears toward interstellar visitors passing through our solar

229
00:16:03,108 --> 00:16:03,609
system.

230
00:16:04,133 --> 00:16:08,735
The result, so far, is the same as Drake's in nineteen sixty.

231
00:16:08,735 --> 00:16:09,339
Silence.

232
00:16:09,867 --> 00:16:12,010
But is that silence meaningful?

233
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:17,542
The astronomer Jill Tarter, one of the pioneers of modern SETI, offered a famous

234
00:16:17,542 --> 00:16:18,303
comparison.

235
00:16:18,303 --> 00:16:23,696
Our search so far, she said, is like scooping a single glass of water from the

236
00:16:23,696 --> 00:16:24,111
ocean.

237
00:16:24,111 --> 00:16:29,850
If you found no fish in that glass, you would not conclude that the ocean is empty.

238
00:16:29,850 --> 00:16:34,414
In two thousand eighteen, a team led by Jason Wright recalculated.

239
00:16:34,414 --> 00:16:40,015
The amount searched had grown, but only to about one large hot tub, compared with

240
00:16:40,015 --> 00:16:41,675
all the oceans of Earth.

241
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:45,315
So perhaps the silence means nothing yet.

242
00:16:45,315 --> 00:16:47,975
And yet, there is a deeper problem.

243
00:16:47,975 --> 00:16:51,926
The Fermi Paradox is not really about radio signals.

244
00:16:51,926 --> 00:16:54,509
It is about something much larger.

245
00:17:06,533 --> 00:17:12,445
Imagine a civilization just a little ahead of us, sending out spacecraft at one

246
00:17:12,445 --> 00:17:14,316
tenth the speed of light.

247
00:17:14,316 --> 00:17:20,377
Not science fiction physics, just a modest extension of technology we can already

248
00:17:20,377 --> 00:17:20,976
imagine.

249
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:26,139
When a ship arrives at a new star system, it builds a settlement.

250
00:17:26,139 --> 00:17:30,849
After a few centuries, that settlement sends out ships of its own.

251
00:17:30,849 --> 00:17:36,630
Even with long pauses at every stop, the wave of expansion would cross the entire

252
00:17:36,630 --> 00:17:42,125
Milky Way, about one hundred thousand light years, in somewhere between a few

253
00:17:42,125 --> 00:17:45,265
million and a few tens of millions of years.

254
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:47,939
That sounds like a long time.

255
00:17:47,939 --> 00:17:48,677
It is not.

256
00:17:48,677 --> 00:17:52,291
The galaxy is roughly thirteen billion years old.

257
00:17:52,291 --> 00:17:55,684
Earth has existed for four and a half billion.

258
00:17:55,684 --> 00:18:01,733
If you compressed the galaxy's history into a single year, the colonization of the

259
00:18:01,733 --> 00:18:04,979
entire Milky Way would take less than a day.

260
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:09,553
And a civilization does not even need to travel itself.

261
00:18:09,553 --> 00:18:15,375
The mathematician John von Neumann imagined machines that could build copies of

262
00:18:15,375 --> 00:18:16,185
themselves.

263
00:18:16,185 --> 00:18:22,302
A self replicating probe could arrive at a star, mine an asteroid, build two copies

264
00:18:22,302 --> 00:18:24,660
of itself, and send them onward.

265
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:30,408
Within a few million years, there could be a probe in every star system of the

266
00:18:30,408 --> 00:18:32,103
galaxy, including ours.

267
00:18:32,633 --> 00:18:37,412
Advanced civilizations might also be visible simply by their size.

268
00:18:37,412 --> 00:18:43,494
In nineteen sixty, the physicist Freeman Dyson suggested that a growing civilization

269
00:18:43,494 --> 00:18:49,359
would eventually want all the energy of its star, and might surround it with vast

270
00:18:49,359 --> 00:18:54,210
collecting structures, a swarm of panels now called a Dyson sphere.

271
00:18:54,210 --> 00:19:00,292
Such a star would glow strangely in infrared light, the waste heat of a civilization

272
00:19:00,292 --> 00:19:01,668
impossible to hide.

273
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:08,714
In nineteen sixty four, the Soviet astronomer Nikolai Kardashev proposed a scale for

274
00:19:08,714 --> 00:19:11,041
civilizations based on energy.

275
00:19:11,041 --> 00:19:16,315
A Type One civilization uses all the energy available on its planet.

276
00:19:16,315 --> 00:19:19,882
Type Two harnesses the full power of its star.

277
00:19:19,882 --> 00:19:23,837
Type Three commands the energy of an entire galaxy.

278
00:19:23,837 --> 00:19:28,568
Humanity, by most calculations, has not yet reached Type One.

279
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:34,721
A Type Three civilization would be visible across millions of light years.

280
00:19:34,721 --> 00:19:36,545
Astronomers have looked.

281
00:19:36,545 --> 00:19:42,546
Surveys of tens of thousands of galaxies with infrared telescopes have found no

282
00:19:42,546 --> 00:19:46,572
clear sign of galaxies being consumed by engineering.

283
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:50,247
That is the true force of Fermi's question.

284
00:19:50,247 --> 00:19:53,028
It is not just that no one has called.

285
00:19:53,028 --> 00:19:56,907
It is that no one seems to have done anything at all.

286
00:19:56,907 --> 00:19:59,103
No probes in our solar system.

287
00:19:59,103 --> 00:19:59,908
No beacons.

288
00:19:59,908 --> 00:20:02,396
No stars dimmed by megastructures.

289
00:20:02,396 --> 00:20:08,104
In a galaxy billions of years old, with billions of habitable worlds, even one

290
00:20:08,104 --> 00:20:12,935
expansionist civilization should have left its mark on everything.

291
00:20:13,467 --> 00:20:18,719
And yet, everywhere we look, the universe appears natural, untouched, empty.

292
00:20:18,719 --> 00:20:22,659
Something, somewhere, must be wrong with our assumptions.

293
00:20:22,659 --> 00:20:26,875
Over the decades, scientists have proposed dozens of answers.

294
00:20:26,875 --> 00:20:29,363
They fall into three broad families.

295
00:20:29,363 --> 00:20:30,815
Perhaps life is rare.

296
00:20:30,815 --> 00:20:32,888
Perhaps something destroys it.

297
00:20:32,888 --> 00:20:37,104
Or perhaps they are out there, and we simply cannot see them.

298
00:20:49,133 --> 00:20:55,173
In the year two thousand, the paleontologist Peter Ward and the astronomer Donald

299
00:20:55,173 --> 00:20:59,795
Brownlee published a book that challenged decades of optimism.

300
00:20:59,795 --> 00:21:01,659
It was called Rare Earth.

301
00:21:01,659 --> 00:21:07,698
Their argument was simple: simple life might be common, but complex life, animals

302
00:21:07,698 --> 00:21:11,128
and intelligence, may be extraordinarily rare.

303
00:21:11,667 --> 00:21:15,235
Consider everything that had to go right for us.

304
00:21:15,235 --> 00:21:21,257
Our Sun sits in a relatively calm region of the galaxy, far from the crowded core

305
00:21:21,257 --> 00:21:26,536
where supernova explosions and intense radiation may sterilize planets.

306
00:21:27,067 --> 00:21:29,790
Our Sun itself is unusually stable.

307
00:21:29,790 --> 00:21:36,169
Many stars, especially the common red dwarfs, erupt with violent flares that could

308
00:21:36,169 --> 00:21:38,736
strip away a planet's atmosphere.

309
00:21:39,267 --> 00:21:45,249
Our solar system has Jupiter, a giant that may have shaped the delivery of water and

310
00:21:45,249 --> 00:21:48,739
helped stabilize the orbits of the inner planets.

311
00:21:49,267 --> 00:21:55,384
We have a large Moon, born from a colossal collision, which may help stabilize the

312
00:21:55,384 --> 00:22:00,531
tilt of Earth's axis and with it our climate over long spans of time.

313
00:22:01,067 --> 00:22:07,295
Earth has plate tectonics, a slow geological engine that recycles carbon and helps

314
00:22:07,295 --> 00:22:11,548
regulate temperature over hundreds of millions of years.

315
00:22:11,548 --> 00:22:16,789
It has a magnetic field that shields the surface from the solar wind.

316
00:22:17,333 --> 00:22:19,470
And then there is life itself.

317
00:22:19,470 --> 00:22:25,097
For roughly two billion years, life on Earth consisted of single, simple cells.

318
00:22:25,097 --> 00:22:31,080
Then, in an event that seems to have happened only once in the entire history of the

319
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:36,208
planet, one cell engulfed another, and instead of digesting it, formed a

320
00:22:36,208 --> 00:22:37,062
partnership.

321
00:22:37,062 --> 00:22:42,832
That accident gave rise to the complex cell, the ancestor of every plant, animal,

322
00:22:42,832 --> 00:22:44,256
and fungus on Earth.

323
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:51,039
Even after that, it took more than a billion additional years for complex animals to

324
00:22:51,039 --> 00:22:51,559
appear.

325
00:22:51,559 --> 00:22:57,724
And of the billions of species that have ever lived on Earth, exactly one has built

326
00:22:57,724 --> 00:22:58,987
radio telescopes.

327
00:22:59,533 --> 00:23:02,823
Our story was shaped by chance at every turn.

328
00:23:02,823 --> 00:23:08,819
Sixty six million years ago, an asteroid struck what is now Mexico, ending the age

329
00:23:08,819 --> 00:23:12,474
of the dinosaurs and clearing the way for mammals.

330
00:23:12,474 --> 00:23:18,177
Had it arrived half an hour earlier or later, striking deep ocean instead, the

331
00:23:18,177 --> 00:23:22,637
history of life might have taken a completely different path.

332
00:23:23,167 --> 00:23:29,312
Perhaps intelligence is not an inevitable destination of evolution, but a bizarre

333
00:23:29,312 --> 00:23:30,981
and improbable detour.

334
00:23:30,981 --> 00:23:37,203
Perhaps the galaxy is full of microbial slime, oceans of simple cells on countless

335
00:23:37,203 --> 00:23:41,300
worlds, and nothing that will ever look up and wonder.

336
00:23:41,833 --> 00:23:46,952
Critics point out that the Rare Earth argument rests on a sample of one.

337
00:23:46,952 --> 00:23:51,501
We do not know how many of these conditions are truly necessary.

338
00:23:51,501 --> 00:23:55,127
Life elsewhere might find entirely different paths.

339
00:23:55,127 --> 00:24:00,956
But if Ward and Brownlee are right, then the silence of the universe has a simple,

340
00:24:00,956 --> 00:24:03,444
and profoundly lonely, explanation.

341
00:24:15,467 --> 00:24:21,396
In nineteen ninety six, the economist Robin Hanson framed the paradox in a new and

342
00:24:21,396 --> 00:24:22,336
chilling way.

343
00:24:22,867 --> 00:24:29,278
Imagine all the steps between a lifeless planet and a civilization that spreads

344
00:24:29,278 --> 00:24:30,658
across the stars.

345
00:24:30,658 --> 00:24:31,875
The right star.

346
00:24:31,875 --> 00:24:33,255
The right planet.

347
00:24:33,255 --> 00:24:34,797
The origin of life.

348
00:24:34,797 --> 00:24:36,177
The complex cell.

349
00:24:36,177 --> 00:24:37,800
Sexual reproduction.

350
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:39,342
Multicellular life.

351
00:24:39,342 --> 00:24:41,209
Tools and intelligence.

352
00:24:41,209 --> 00:24:42,101
Technology.

353
00:24:42,101 --> 00:24:45,267
And finally, expansion into the galaxy.

354
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:51,531
Given how many planets exist, and how empty the universe appears, at least one of

355
00:24:51,531 --> 00:24:54,857
these steps must be extraordinarily improbable.

356
00:24:54,857 --> 00:25:00,376
Somewhere along the path, there is a barrier that almost nothing gets through.

357
00:25:00,376 --> 00:25:02,781
Hanson called it the Great Filter.

358
00:25:03,300 --> 00:25:06,612
The crucial question is: where is that filter?

359
00:25:06,612 --> 00:25:08,484
Is it behind us, or ahead?

360
00:25:09,033 --> 00:25:15,261
If the Great Filter is behind us, perhaps at the origin of life, or at the birth of

361
00:25:15,261 --> 00:25:19,387
the complex cell, then we are among the rare survivors.

362
00:25:19,387 --> 00:25:23,363
We passed a test that nearly every other world fails.

363
00:25:23,363 --> 00:25:28,240
The galaxy is empty because almost no one gets as far as we have.

364
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:31,241
And the future could be ours to explore.

365
00:25:31,767 --> 00:25:37,694
But if the Great Filter lies ahead of us, then civilizations like ours are common,

366
00:25:37,694 --> 00:25:42,031
and something stops all of them before they reach the stars.

367
00:25:42,031 --> 00:25:42,754
Every one.

368
00:25:42,754 --> 00:25:44,055
Without exception.

369
00:25:44,600 --> 00:25:46,766
What could such a filter be?

370
00:25:46,766 --> 00:25:53,030
Since the middle of the twentieth century, humanity has lived with the ability to

371
00:25:53,030 --> 00:25:54,191
destroy itself.

372
00:25:54,191 --> 00:25:55,119
Nuclear war.

373
00:25:55,119 --> 00:25:56,743
Engineered pandemics.

374
00:25:56,743 --> 00:25:58,522
Runaway climate change.

375
00:25:58,522 --> 00:26:04,864
Technologies we do not yet understand, from artificial intelligence to weapons not

376
00:26:04,864 --> 00:26:05,869
yet imagined.

377
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:12,059
This is why the philosopher Nick Bostrom once wrote that he hoped our searches for

378
00:26:12,059 --> 00:26:14,268
life on Mars would find nothing.

379
00:26:14,268 --> 00:26:20,065
If we discovered that simple life had arisen independently on Mars, it would suggest

380
00:26:20,065 --> 00:26:24,827
that life starts easily, and that the early steps are not the filter.

381
00:26:24,827 --> 00:26:29,589
The barrier would then be more likely to lie somewhere in our future.

382
00:26:30,133 --> 00:26:36,685
In Bostrom's view, finding fossils on Mars would be the worst news in the history of

383
00:26:36,685 --> 00:26:37,387
humanity.

384
00:26:37,387 --> 00:26:42,613
Dead rocks and lifeless sands, by contrast, would lift our spirits.

385
00:26:43,133 --> 00:26:44,956
It is a strange thought.

386
00:26:44,956 --> 00:26:50,881
Every discovery that makes life in the universe seem easier also makes our own

387
00:26:50,881 --> 00:26:52,324
future look darker.

388
00:26:52,324 --> 00:26:56,957
And every silence makes us look more special, or more doomed.

389
00:26:57,500 --> 00:27:00,221
We do not know where the filter lies.

390
00:27:00,221 --> 00:27:02,134
Perhaps there are several.

391
00:27:02,134 --> 00:27:06,694
Perhaps it is not a single wall, but a long series of hurdles.

392
00:27:06,694 --> 00:27:12,284
But the idea forces us to ask something uncomfortable: are we looking at the

393
00:27:12,284 --> 00:27:14,417
universe, or at our own fate?

394
00:27:26,467 --> 00:27:28,432
There is a third possibility.

395
00:27:28,432 --> 00:27:32,227
Perhaps they are out there, and the silence is a choice.

396
00:27:32,767 --> 00:27:38,667
In nineteen seventy three, the astronomer John Ball proposed what he called the Zoo

397
00:27:38,667 --> 00:27:39,449
Hypothesis.

398
00:27:39,449 --> 00:27:45,350
Perhaps advanced civilizations know about us, but have chosen not to interfere, the

399
00:27:45,350 --> 00:27:50,397
way we might watch animals in a nature reserve without disturbing them.

400
00:27:50,397 --> 00:27:56,297
Earth might be a protected wilderness, observed but left alone, until we are ready.

401
00:27:56,833 --> 00:28:00,390
A far darker version comes from science fiction.

402
00:28:00,390 --> 00:28:06,540
In his novel The Dark Forest, the Chinese author Liu Cixin imagined the universe as

403
00:28:06,540 --> 00:28:09,948
a forest at night, filled with silent hunters.

404
00:28:09,948 --> 00:28:12,690
Every civilization fears every other.

405
00:28:12,690 --> 00:28:18,692
Since no one can know another's intentions, and since technology can leap forward

406
00:28:18,692 --> 00:28:24,545
unpredictably, the safest strategy is to stay hidden, and to destroy anyone who

407
00:28:24,545 --> 00:28:25,953
reveals themselves.

408
00:28:26,500 --> 00:28:32,266
In that universe, broadcasting our existence would be the most dangerous thing a

409
00:28:32,266 --> 00:28:33,924
young species could do.

410
00:28:33,924 --> 00:28:39,402
Some scientists, including Stephen Hawking, have warned against sending loud

411
00:28:39,402 --> 00:28:44,015
messages into space before we understand who might be listening.

412
00:28:44,533 --> 00:28:49,353
Others suggest the silence comes not from fear, but from indifference.

413
00:28:49,353 --> 00:28:54,999
A civilization millions of years older than us might be as different from us as we

414
00:28:54,999 --> 00:28:55,963
are from ants.

415
00:28:55,963 --> 00:28:59,200
We do not try to communicate with ant colonies.

416
00:28:59,200 --> 00:29:02,987
We do not ask their permission when we build a highway.

417
00:29:03,533 --> 00:29:05,986
Then there are more exotic ideas.

418
00:29:05,986 --> 00:29:12,005
The aestivation hypothesis, proposed by Anders Sandberg and colleagues at Oxford,

419
00:29:12,005 --> 00:29:15,944
suggests that advanced civilizations may be sleeping.

420
00:29:15,944 --> 00:29:22,112
Computation becomes more efficient as the universe cools, so a patient civilization

421
00:29:22,112 --> 00:29:28,205
might simply wait, dormant, for trillions of years, preserving its resources for a

422
00:29:28,205 --> 00:29:30,435
colder, more efficient future.

423
00:29:30,967 --> 00:29:36,934
The transcension hypothesis suggests that civilizations do not expand outward at

424
00:29:36,934 --> 00:29:38,127
all, but inward.

425
00:29:38,127 --> 00:29:44,392
As technology advances, they become smaller, denser, more efficient, retreating into

426
00:29:44,392 --> 00:29:50,284
compact, perhaps even black hole like environments, where we would never notice

427
00:29:50,284 --> 00:29:50,657
them.

428
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:57,457
Some philosophers go further still, wondering if our reality is a simulation, and

429
00:29:57,457 --> 00:30:00,392
the silence simply part of its design.

430
00:30:00,392 --> 00:30:04,640
These ideas are difficult, perhaps impossible, to test.

431
00:30:05,167 --> 00:30:07,850
And there is a more humble explanation.

432
00:30:07,850 --> 00:30:12,460
Perhaps they are signaling, but we are not listening the right way.

433
00:30:12,460 --> 00:30:18,171
We search for radio, but advanced beings might use lasers, neutrinos, or methods we

434
00:30:18,171 --> 00:30:19,822
have not yet discovered.

435
00:30:19,822 --> 00:30:22,506
Even our own planet is growing quieter.

436
00:30:22,506 --> 00:30:28,079
As we replace powerful television broadcasts with cables, satellites, and digital

437
00:30:28,079 --> 00:30:31,107
signals, Earth is becoming harder to detect.

438
00:30:31,107 --> 00:30:35,097
A civilization's loud radio age might last only a century.

439
00:30:35,633 --> 00:30:38,537
Each of these solutions has a weakness.

440
00:30:38,537 --> 00:30:44,715
The Zoo Hypothesis requires every civilization, without exception, to obey the same

441
00:30:44,715 --> 00:30:46,725
rule for billions of years.

442
00:30:46,725 --> 00:30:51,043
The Dark Forest requires all of them to be equally afraid.

443
00:30:51,043 --> 00:30:57,222
A single exception, one curious, careless, or expansionist species, would be enough

444
00:30:57,222 --> 00:30:58,636
to fill the galaxy.

445
00:30:58,636 --> 00:31:00,423
And yet, we see nothing.

446
00:31:12,467 --> 00:31:16,947
Perhaps the answer is not about space at all, but about time.

447
00:31:17,467 --> 00:31:20,287
The early universe was a hostile place.

448
00:31:20,287 --> 00:31:24,987
The first stars contained almost nothing but hydrogen and helium.

449
00:31:24,987 --> 00:31:30,845
The heavier elements needed for rocky planets and for life, carbon, oxygen, iron,

450
00:31:30,845 --> 00:31:35,183
had to be forged inside stars and scattered by their deaths.

451
00:31:35,183 --> 00:31:41,041
It took billions of years for galaxies to become rich enough in these elements to

452
00:31:41,041 --> 00:31:42,704
build worlds like ours.

453
00:31:43,233 --> 00:31:49,439
Violent events like gamma ray bursts, the most powerful explosions known, may have

454
00:31:49,439 --> 00:31:53,752
regularly sterilized large parts of galaxies in the past.

455
00:31:53,752 --> 00:31:59,958
Some researchers suggest that only in the last few billion years has the Milky Way

456
00:31:59,958 --> 00:32:04,801
become calm enough for complex life to survive for long periods.

457
00:32:05,333 --> 00:32:07,709
If so, we may be among the first.

458
00:32:07,709 --> 00:32:09,796
Not alone forever, but early.

459
00:32:09,796 --> 00:32:13,611
One of the first lights to switch on in a dark house.

460
00:32:14,133 --> 00:32:20,181
In two thousand twenty one, Robin Hanson and his colleagues developed this idea into

461
00:32:20,181 --> 00:32:23,132
what they called the Grabby Aliens model.

462
00:32:23,132 --> 00:32:24,932
Their argument is subtle.

463
00:32:24,932 --> 00:32:30,476
Some civilizations, they suggest, eventually expand rapidly, at a significant

464
00:32:30,476 --> 00:32:35,299
fraction of the speed of light, transforming everything they reach.

465
00:32:35,299 --> 00:32:40,843
Once a region of space has been claimed, no new civilization can arise there.

466
00:32:41,367 --> 00:32:46,911
This means that anyone who finds themselves still able to look up at a natural,

467
00:32:46,911 --> 00:32:48,876
untouched sky must be early.

468
00:32:48,876 --> 00:32:54,772
If we had arisen later, the grabby civilizations would already be here, and we would

469
00:32:54,772 --> 00:32:56,947
not exist to wonder about them.

470
00:32:56,947 --> 00:33:02,772
By their calculations, we may meet such civilizations hundreds of millions of years

471
00:33:02,772 --> 00:33:03,404
from now.

472
00:33:03,933 --> 00:33:10,238
In two thousand eighteen, researchers at Oxford, Anders Sandberg, Eric Drexler, and

473
00:33:10,238 --> 00:33:12,669
Toby Ord, took another approach.

474
00:33:12,669 --> 00:33:18,442
They noticed that most people plug single, best guess numbers into the Drake

475
00:33:18,442 --> 00:33:19,126
Equation.

476
00:33:19,126 --> 00:33:25,203
But our real uncertainty about some factors spans dozens of orders of magnitude.

477
00:33:25,733 --> 00:33:31,568
When they properly accounted for that uncertainty, the picture changed dramatically.

478
00:33:31,568 --> 00:33:37,264
By their estimate, there was a substantial probability, quite possibly better than

479
00:33:37,264 --> 00:33:42,543
even, that we are the only intelligent civilization in the entire observable

480
00:33:42,543 --> 00:33:43,168
universe.

481
00:33:43,168 --> 00:33:46,919
The paradox, they argued, may not be a paradox at all.

482
00:33:46,919 --> 00:33:51,781
The silence may simply be what an uncertain universe should look like.

483
00:33:52,300 --> 00:33:53,838
Distance matters too.

484
00:33:53,838 --> 00:33:57,864
The Milky Way is a hundred thousand light years across.

485
00:33:57,864 --> 00:34:03,429
Even if a thousand civilizations existed right now, they would on average be

486
00:34:03,429 --> 00:34:06,577
separated by many thousands of light years.

487
00:34:06,577 --> 00:34:12,141
A signal we send today might not be answered for tens of thousands of years.

488
00:34:12,141 --> 00:34:17,047
Civilizations could rise and fall without ever overlapping in time.

489
00:34:17,567 --> 00:34:23,203
Like two fireflies blinking in a vast field, flashing at different moments, never

490
00:34:23,203 --> 00:34:25,012
seeing each other's light.

491
00:34:37,033 --> 00:34:42,811
On October nineteenth, two thousand seventeen, a telescope in Hawaii spotted

492
00:34:42,811 --> 00:34:46,004
something racing through the solar system.

493
00:34:46,004 --> 00:34:49,500
It was moving too fast to be bound to the Sun.

494
00:34:49,500 --> 00:34:52,237
It had come from interstellar space.

495
00:34:52,237 --> 00:34:58,623
It was named Oumuamua, a Hawaiian word meaning a messenger from afar arriving first.

496
00:34:59,167 --> 00:35:00,857
Oumuamua was strange.

497
00:35:00,857 --> 00:35:06,895
It appeared to be highly elongated, perhaps like a cigar or a flat pancake.

498
00:35:06,895 --> 00:35:11,242
It showed no visible tail of gas or dust like a comet.

499
00:35:11,242 --> 00:35:17,762
And yet, as it left, it accelerated slightly, as if something were gently pushing

500
00:35:17,762 --> 00:35:18,004
it.

501
00:35:18,533 --> 00:35:25,203
Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb suggested that Oumuamua might be an artificial object,

502
00:35:25,203 --> 00:35:28,457
perhaps a light sail pushed by sunlight.

503
00:35:28,457 --> 00:35:35,126
Most astronomers disagreed, proposing natural explanations, such as gases escaping

504
00:35:35,126 --> 00:35:37,322
invisibly from an icy body.

505
00:35:37,322 --> 00:35:41,552
The object was gone before anyone could look closer.

506
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:48,269
In two thousand nineteen, a second interstellar visitor arrived, the comet Borisov,

507
00:35:48,269 --> 00:35:50,128
a clearly natural object.

508
00:35:50,128 --> 00:35:56,223
And in July of two thousand twenty five, a third: an object named three I ATLAS, a

509
00:35:56,223 --> 00:36:02,244
large comet, possibly older than our own solar system, swept in from the galaxy's

510
00:36:02,244 --> 00:36:03,656
ancient thick disk.

511
00:36:03,656 --> 00:36:08,785
Telescopes and spacecraft across the solar system turned to study it.

512
00:36:08,785 --> 00:36:12,799
Radio searches, as always, found no artificial signal.

513
00:36:13,333 --> 00:36:19,325
In two thousand fifteen, citizen scientists studying Kepler data found a star, KIC

514
00:36:19,325 --> 00:36:25,097
eight four six two eight five two, later nicknamed Tabby's Star, that dimmed in

515
00:36:25,097 --> 00:36:29,700
bizarre, irregular ways, sometimes by more than twenty percent.

516
00:36:29,700 --> 00:36:33,719
For a time, some speculated about alien megastructures.

517
00:36:33,719 --> 00:36:39,637
Further observations showed that the dimming changed with the color of light, the

518
00:36:39,637 --> 00:36:42,560
signature of dust, not solid structures.

519
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:48,773
In two thousand twenty four, a project called Hephaistos searched millions of stars

520
00:36:48,773 --> 00:36:51,986
for the infrared glow of partial Dyson spheres.

521
00:36:51,986 --> 00:36:57,112
It found seven candidates, all small red dwarf stars with unexplained heat.

522
00:36:57,112 --> 00:37:02,717
Follow up work suggested that at least some of them could be explained by distant,

523
00:37:02,717 --> 00:37:06,134
dusty background galaxies hiding behind the stars.

524
00:37:06,134 --> 00:37:08,663
But the search itself marked a shift.

525
00:37:08,663 --> 00:37:14,268
Astronomers now look not only for messages, but for the footprints of engineering.

526
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:19,080
Meanwhile, the hunt for simple life has moved into a new era.

527
00:37:19,080 --> 00:37:24,903
In two thousand twenty three and again in two thousand twenty five, a team from the

528
00:37:24,903 --> 00:37:30,726
University of Cambridge used the James Webb Space Telescope to study the atmosphere

529
00:37:30,726 --> 00:37:36,198
of K2 eighteen b, a planet about one hundred and twenty four light years away.

530
00:37:36,198 --> 00:37:42,092
They reported hints of dimethyl sulfide, a molecule that on Earth is produced mainly

531
00:37:42,092 --> 00:37:45,179
by living organisms, such as ocean plankton.

532
00:37:45,700 --> 00:37:48,999
The announcement made headlines around the world.

533
00:37:48,999 --> 00:37:54,182
But other teams soon reanalyzed the data, and found the evidence was far from

534
00:37:54,182 --> 00:37:54,923
conclusive.

535
00:37:54,923 --> 00:38:00,578
The signal fell well short of the standard required for a discovery, and some argued

536
00:38:00,578 --> 00:38:04,146
that dimethyl sulfide could be produced without life.

537
00:38:04,146 --> 00:38:07,849
The debate continues, and more observations are needed.

538
00:38:07,849 --> 00:38:13,234
But for the first time in history, we are close to being able to read the air of

539
00:38:13,234 --> 00:38:14,244
distant worlds.

540
00:38:14,767 --> 00:38:16,509
And then there is Mars.

541
00:38:16,509 --> 00:38:22,719
In July two thousand twenty four, NASA's Perseverance rover drilled into a reddish

542
00:38:22,719 --> 00:38:28,021
rock nicknamed Cheyava Falls, in an ancient riverbed in Jezero Crater.

543
00:38:28,021 --> 00:38:34,383
The rock was speckled with tiny spots, nicknamed leopard spots, rimmed with minerals

544
00:38:34,383 --> 00:38:38,852
that on Earth are often associated with microbial activity.

545
00:38:39,400 --> 00:38:44,693
In September two thousand twenty five, after a year of analysis, scientists

546
00:38:44,693 --> 00:38:47,939
published their results in the journal Nature.

547
00:38:47,939 --> 00:38:53,020
The sample, named Sapphire Canyon, contains what NASA called a potential

548
00:38:53,020 --> 00:38:58,878
biosignature, the most compelling candidate for ancient life the mission has found.

549
00:38:58,878 --> 00:39:02,195
Non biological explanations are still possible.

550
00:39:02,195 --> 00:39:07,770
The sample awaits a journey back to Earth, where laboratories could study it in

551
00:39:07,770 --> 00:39:08,264
detail.

552
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:14,856
Elsewhere, scientists continue to debate a possible detection of phosphine in the

553
00:39:14,856 --> 00:39:18,520
clouds of Venus, reported in two thousand twenty.

554
00:39:18,520 --> 00:39:24,652
Others look to the hidden oceans of Europa and Enceladus, moons where liquid water

555
00:39:24,652 --> 00:39:30,783
lies beneath miles of ice, warmed from within, and where jets of water vapor spray

556
00:39:30,783 --> 00:39:31,605
into space.

557
00:39:32,133 --> 00:39:36,125
None of these clues has yet proven that we are not alone.

558
00:39:36,125 --> 00:39:41,938
But they show that the question is moving, for the first time, from philosophy into

559
00:39:41,938 --> 00:39:42,779
measurement.

560
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:58,233
We are living at the beginning of a revolution.

561
00:39:58,233 --> 00:40:04,368
The James Webb Space Telescope is already measuring the atmospheres of rocky planets

562
00:40:04,368 --> 00:40:05,829
around nearby stars.

563
00:40:06,367 --> 00:40:12,138
In the Atacama Desert of Chile, astronomers are building the Extremely Large

564
00:40:12,138 --> 00:40:17,832
Telescope, with a mirror thirty nine meters across, larger than any optical

565
00:40:17,832 --> 00:40:19,427
telescope ever built.

566
00:40:19,427 --> 00:40:25,653
It may be able to directly image rocky planets around the closest stars and search

567
00:40:25,653 --> 00:40:28,767
their air for oxygen, water, and methane.

568
00:40:29,300 --> 00:40:35,549
The Vera Rubin Observatory has begun a ten year survey of the entire southern sky,

569
00:40:35,549 --> 00:40:41,341
watching for anything that moves or changes, including the next interstellar

570
00:40:41,341 --> 00:40:41,951
visitor.

571
00:40:42,500 --> 00:40:48,605
The Square Kilometre Array, being built in Australia and South Africa, will be the

572
00:40:48,605 --> 00:40:54,636
largest radio observatory on Earth, sensitive enough to detect airport radar from

573
00:40:54,636 --> 00:40:57,241
planets dozens of light years away.

574
00:40:57,767 --> 00:41:03,834
NASA's Europa Clipper is already on its way to Jupiter's ocean moon, scheduled to

575
00:41:03,834 --> 00:41:06,081
arrive in two thousand thirty.

576
00:41:06,081 --> 00:41:12,074
Later this decade, the Dragonfly mission is planned to launch to Titan, Saturn's

577
00:41:12,074 --> 00:41:17,842
largest moon, a world of methane lakes and organic chemistry, where a nuclear

578
00:41:17,842 --> 00:41:20,913
powered drone will fly from site to site.

579
00:41:21,433 --> 00:41:27,593
And NASA is planning a future flagship, the Habitable Worlds Observatory, a space

580
00:41:27,593 --> 00:41:33,525
telescope designed with one primary goal: to directly image Earth like planets

581
00:41:33,525 --> 00:41:39,076
around Sun like stars, and to search their atmospheres for signs of life.

582
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:44,997
For the first time, humanity is building the tools that could answer Fermi's

583
00:41:44,997 --> 00:41:47,412
question within our own lifetimes.

584
00:41:47,412 --> 00:41:49,968
Not with speculation, but with data.

585
00:41:50,500 --> 00:41:53,124
And whatever we find, it will change us.

586
00:42:05,167 --> 00:42:11,274
On February fourteenth, nineteen ninety, at the request of Carl Sagan, the Voyager

587
00:42:11,274 --> 00:42:17,530
one spacecraft turned its camera back toward home, from about six billion kilometers

588
00:42:17,530 --> 00:42:17,902
away.

589
00:42:17,902 --> 00:42:24,084
In the resulting image, Earth is barely a single pixel, a tiny speck suspended in a

590
00:42:24,084 --> 00:42:26,095
scattered beam of sunlight.

591
00:42:26,633 --> 00:42:29,003
Sagan called it a pale blue dot.

592
00:42:29,003 --> 00:42:34,556
Everyone you have ever loved, every person who ever lived, every war, every

593
00:42:34,556 --> 00:42:39,220
discovery, every hope, all of it happened on that mote of dust.

594
00:42:39,767 --> 00:42:41,356
The writer Arthur C.

595
00:42:41,356 --> 00:42:45,967
Clarke is often quoted as saying: two possibilities exist.

596
00:42:45,967 --> 00:42:50,021
Either we are alone in the universe, or we are not.

597
00:42:50,021 --> 00:42:52,247
Both are equally terrifying.

598
00:42:52,767 --> 00:42:58,284
If we are not alone, then somewhere out there are minds shaped by other worlds,

599
00:42:58,284 --> 00:43:00,868
other histories, other ways of being.

600
00:43:00,868 --> 00:43:03,242
Perhaps one day we will hear them.

601
00:43:03,242 --> 00:43:06,036
Perhaps one day we will understand them.

602
00:43:06,567 --> 00:43:11,703
And if we are alone, then something rare and precious has happened here.

603
00:43:11,703 --> 00:43:17,482
In all the vastness of space and time, the universe has, in this one small place,

604
00:43:17,482 --> 00:43:19,123
become aware of itself.

605
00:43:19,123 --> 00:43:24,759
Then every life, every species, every forest and ocean on this planet carries a

606
00:43:24,759 --> 00:43:27,256
weight almost too large to imagine.

607
00:43:27,256 --> 00:43:32,892
If we are the only ones, then we are the universe's only chance to know itself.

608
00:43:33,433 --> 00:43:37,802
Either way, the answer calls us to the same responsibility.

609
00:43:37,802 --> 00:43:38,616
To survive.

610
00:43:38,616 --> 00:43:40,837
To protect this fragile world.

611
00:43:40,837 --> 00:43:43,503
To keep looking, and keep listening.

612
00:43:44,033 --> 00:43:50,018
More than seventy five years ago, Enrico Fermi asked a simple question over lunch.

613
00:43:50,018 --> 00:43:52,353
We still do not know the answer.

614
00:43:54,900 --> 00:43:58,292
But we are, at last, close enough to search for it.

615
00:44:00,833 --> 00:44:02,775
So, where is everybody?

616
00:44:05,300 --> 00:44:08,521
Perhaps they are asking the same question about us.

